Below is a compact, but fully‑functional C# example that shows:

1. **A generic class** (`Pair<T1,T2>`) that can hold any two values.  
2. **A generic method** (`Swap<T>`) that works on any type.  
3. **Generic constraints** (`where T : IComparable<T>`) so the method can only be used with types that can be compared.  
4. **Usage** that demonstrates the above with built‑in types (`int`, `string`) and a custom type (`Person`).

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

// 1. Generic class that can hold any two values.
public class Pair<T1, T2>
{
    public T1 First  { get; set; }
    public T2 Second { get; set; }

    public Pair(T1 first, T2 second)
    {
        First  = first;
        Second = second;
    }

    public override string ToString() =>
        $"({First}, {Second})";
}

// 2. Generic method that swaps two values of any type.
public static class Utilities
{
    public static void Swap<T>(ref T a, ref T b)
    {
        T temp = a;
        a = b;
        b = temp;
    }

    // 3. Generic method with a constraint: T must be comparable.
    public static T Max<T>(T a, T b) where T : IComparable<T>
    {
        return a.CompareTo(b) > 0 ? a : b;
    }
}

// 4. Custom type that implements IComparable so it can be used with Max<T>.
public class Person : IComparable<Person>
{
    public string Name { get; }
    public int    Age  { get; }

    public Person(string name, int age)
    {
        Name = name;
        Age  = age;
    }

    // Compare by Age.
    public int CompareTo(Person other)
    {
        if (other == null) return 1;
        return Age.CompareTo(other.Age);
    }

    public override string ToString() => $"{Name} ({Age})";
}

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        // Using the generic Pair class
        var intStringPair = new Pair<int, string>(42, "Answer");
        Console.WriteLine(intStringPair); // Output: (42, Answer)

        // Using the generic Swap method
        int x = 5, y = 10;
        Console.WriteLine($"Before swap: x={x}, y={y}");
        Utilities.Swap(ref x, ref y);
        Console.WriteLine($"After swap:  x={x}, y={y}");

        // Using Max<T> with built‑in types
        int maxInt = Utilities.Max(7, 13);
        Console.WriteLine($"Max int: {maxInt}");

        string maxString = Utilities.Max("apple", "banana");
        Console.WriteLine($"Max string: {maxString}");   // Lexicographic comparison

        // Using Max<T> with a custom type
        Person p1 = new Person("Alice", 30);
        Person p2 = new Person("Bob",   25);
        Person older = Utilities.Max(p1, p2);
        Console.WriteLine($"Older person: {older}");
    }
}
```

### What this code demonstrates

| Feature | How it’s shown |
|---------|----------------|
| **Generic class** | `Pair<T1,T2>` can store any two values (`int` & `string`, `Person` & `int`, etc.). |
| **Generic method** | `Swap<T>` works on any type without writing separate overloads. |
| **Generic constraints** | `Max<T>` only accepts types that implement `IComparable<T>`. |
| **Custom type usage** | `Person` implements `IComparable<Person>`, allowing it to be used with `Max<T>`. |
| **Ref parameters** | `Swap<T>` uses `ref` to modify the arguments in place. |

Compile and run the program; you’ll see output that reflects each of the features above. This small example encapsulates the core concepts of C# generics in a single, self‑contained file.
